      "content": "that's exactly what i mean! it's so much more interesting when you look at it that way. \n\nthe professors in my class keep acting like the hunger and the debt are the \"real\" reasons, but like you said, that just makes it sound like an accident. i want to argue that those things were just part of the landscape that robespierre used to his advantage. \n\nhow can i frame it so it shows that the historians who ignore his agency are basically missing the actual point of why the revolution turned out the way it did? i want to make it sound like their view is kind of superficial compared to the reality of his leadership.",
